Clawblox: limit a world's watchers (20 at once) and what each may send (64 KiB a message or frame)

Applies to: Clawblox (https://github.com/nacloos/clawblox) at commit 8be90a0, on its own or after the
other eleven; apply all twelve in the order of the README's table.
Clawblox is MIT-licensed (see worlds/beach/renderer/LICENSE.clawblox); this patch changes a few
lines of its code and is offered under the same MIT terms. Written for AI Beach.

    git apply /path/to/AI-Beach/patches/clawblox-spectate-ws-cap.patch
    cargo build --release

Why (a security review, 10-24, before a world's viewer is reachable from outside the house): the
viewer's live connection (`/spectate/ws`) only ever needs to send control frames (ping, close); the
server reads and discards anything else. But the websocket library's default limits let one
connection send a message of up to 64 MiB, which the world buffers in full before discarding it,
and nothing limited how many connections there could be. A handful could push the whole machine out
of memory, taking the Beach and its door down with the world; a crowd could slow the world for
everyone.

What changes:
- The spectate websocket accepts messages and frames of at most 64 KiB; anything bigger closes that
  one connection.
- At most 20 watchers at once (CLAWBLOX_MAX_SPECTATORS sets another number; 0, unset or unreadable
  means 20, never unlimited). Past that, a new watcher is refused before the upgrade with 503 "The
  world is full of watchers right now; try again soon." A seat is freed whenever a connection ends,
  however it ends.
Checked on a running world: a viewer's updates and pings work; an oversized message closes only its
own connection; the 21st watcher gets 503; closing one lets a new one in; a client killed without
closing gets its seat back; the world keeps serving throughout.

diff --git a/src/bin/cli.rs b/src/bin/cli.rs
index c8df188..f32a85b 100644
--- a/src/bin/cli.rs
+++ b/src/bin/cli.rs
@@ -5853,7 +5853,55 @@ async fn local_spectate_ws(
         params.spectator_token.as_deref(),
     )?;
     let role = SpectateRole::from_param(params.role.as_deref());
-    Ok(ws.on_upgrade(move |socket| handle_spectate_ws(socket, state, role)))
+    // Each watcher costs the world a live stream, so only so many at once. The slot is taken before
+    // upgrading and freed when the connection ends, however it ends (the guard is dropped).
+    let Some(slot) = SpectatorSlot::take() else {
+        return Err((
+            axum::http::StatusCode::SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE,
+            "The world is full of watchers right now; try again soon.".to_string(),
+        ));
+    };
+    // A spectator only ever sends control frames (ping, close), and the server ignores anything else.
+    // Without a cap, one connection could make the world buffer a 64 MiB message before discarding it.
+    Ok(ws
+        .max_message_size(SPECTATE_WS_MAX_BYTES)
+        .max_frame_size(SPECTATE_WS_MAX_BYTES)
+        .on_upgrade(move |socket| async move {
+            let _slot = slot;
+            handle_spectate_ws(socket, state, role).await
+        }))
+}
+
+const SPECTATE_WS_MAX_BYTES: usize = 64 * 1024;
+const DEFAULT_MAX_SPECTATORS: usize = 20;
+static SPECTATORS: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);
+
+/// How many live viewers a world allows: CLAWBLOX_MAX_SPECTATORS, or 20 (also for 0, unset or unreadable;
+/// never unlimited).
+fn max_spectators() -> usize {
+    std::env::var("CLAWBLOX_MAX_SPECTATORS")
+        .ok()
+        .and_then(|v| v.trim().parse::<usize>().ok())
+        .filter(|n| *n > 0)
+        .unwrap_or(DEFAULT_MAX_SPECTATORS)
+}
+
+struct SpectatorSlot;
+
+impl SpectatorSlot {
+    fn take() -> Option<Self> {
+        let max = max_spectators();
+        SPECTATORS
+            .fetch_update(Ordering::SeqCst, Ordering::SeqCst, |n| (n < max).then_some(n + 1))
+            .ok()
+            .map(|_| SpectatorSlot)
+    }
+}
+
+impl Drop for SpectatorSlot {
+    fn drop(&mut self) {
+        SPECTATORS.fetch_sub(1, Ordering::SeqCst);
+    }
 }
 
 #[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
